      "text": "To solve the inequality \\(\\frac{x + 6}{x^2 + 2x + 7} \\ge 0\\), we need to consider the critical points and the fact that the denominator \\(x^2 + 2x + 7\\) is always positive for all real numbers (since the discriminant of the quadratic \\(x^2 + 2x + 7\\) is \\(2^2 - 4 \\cdot 1 \\cdot 7 = 4 - 28 = -24\\), which is negative, indicating that the quadratic has no real roots and thus is always positive). Therefore, the only factor affecting the inequality is the numerator \\(x + 6\\).\n\n1. Find the critical points by setting the numerator equal to zero:\n   \\[\n   x + 6 = 0 \\implies x = -6.\n   \\]\n2. Determine the intervals to test. Since the denominator \\(x^2 + 2x + 7\\) is always positive, the inequality will be split into intervals based on the critical point \\(x = -6\\). The intervals to consider are \\((-\\infty, -6)\\) and \\((-6, \\infty)\\).\n\n3. Test each interval:\n   - For \\(x \\in (-\\infty, -6)\\), choose \\(x = -7\\):\n     \\[\n     \\frac{-7 + 6}{(-7)^2 + 2(-7) + 7} = \\frac{-1}{49 - 14 + 7} = \\frac{-1}{38} < 0.\n     \\]\n     So, the inequality is not satisfied in this interval.\n   - For \\(x \\in (-6, \\infty)\\), choose \\(x = 0\\):\n     \\[\n     \\frac{0 + 6}{0^2 + 2 \\cdot 0 + 7} = \\frac{6}{7} > 0.\n     \\]\n     So, the inequality is satisfied in this interval.\n\n4. Since the inequality must also include \\(x = -6\\) as a candidate for a solution (as it makes the numerator zero), and since \\(x = -6\\) is just on the boundary of the interval, we should include \\(-6\\) in the solution set.\n\nTherefore, the solution to the inequality \\(\\frac{x + 6}{x^2 + 2x + 7} \\ge 0\\) is \\((- \\infty, -6] \\cup I\\), where \\(I\\) is the set of all real numbers. The final answer in interval notation is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{(-\\infty, -6] \\cup \\mathbb{R}}.\n\\]",
