      "text": "To solve the inequality \\(\\frac{x + 6}{x^2 + 2x + 7} \\ge 0\\), we need to determine where the fraction \\(\\frac{x + 6}{x^2 + 2x + 7}\\) is non-negative. A fraction is non-negative if its numerator and denominator have the same sign or if the numerator is zero.\n\nStep 1: Find the critical points. The critical points occur where the numerator or the denominator is zero.\n- The numerator \\(x + 6\\) is zero when \\(x = -6\\).\n- The denominator \\(x^2 + 2x + 7\\) is a quadratic equation. To determine where it is zero, we solve \\(x^2 + 2x + 7 = 0\\). We use the quadratic formula \\(x = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a}\\) with \\(a = 1\\), \\(b = 2\\), and \\(c = 7\\).\n\n\\[\nx = \\frac{-2 \\pm \\sqrt{2^2 - 4 \\cdot 1 \\cdot 7}}{2 \\cdot 1} = \\frac{-2 \\pm \\sqrt{4 - 28}}{2} = \\frac{-2 \\pm \\sqrt{-24}}{2} = \\frac{-2 \\pm 2\\sqrt{-6}}{2} = -1 \\pm \\sqrt{-6}\n\\]\n\nThe roots are complex numbers, \\(-1 + i\\sqrt{6}\\) and \\(-1 - i\\sqrt{6}\\), which means the quadratic \\(x^2 + 2x + 7\\) has no real roots and is always positive (since the discriminant is negative).\n\nStep 2: Determine the intervals where the function is non-negative. Since the denominator is always positive, the non-negative portion of the fraction depends on the numerator.\n\n- When \\(x^2 + 2x + 7 > 0\\), the fraction \\(\\frac{x + 6}{x^2 + 2x + 7}\\) is non-negative for all real \\(x\\). Thus, the solution is all real numbers except where the denominator is zero (which it is never zero).\n\nTherefore, the solution is all real numbers except \\(x = -6\\).\n\nIn interval notation, the solution is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{(-\\infty, -6) \\cup (-6, \\infty)}\n\\]",
