      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To solve the inequality \\(\\frac{x + 6}{x^2 + 2x + 7} \\ge 0\\), we need to determine the intervals where the expression is non-negative. This involves finding the roots of the numerator and denominator and analyzing the sign of the expression in the intervals defined by these roots.\n\n1. **Factorize the denominator:**\n   The denominator \\(x^2 + 2x + 7\\) can be rewritten using a completing the square method:\n   \\[\n   x^2 + 2x + 7 = (x + 1)^2 + 6\n   \\]\n   Since \\((x + 1)^2\\) is always non-negative, \\((x + 1)^2 + 6\\) is always positive for all real values of \\(x\\).\n\n2. **Determine the roots of the numerator:**\n   The numerator \\(x + 6\\) is a linear expression, and it is a constant term. Setting it to zero, we have:\n   \\[\n   x + 6 = 0 \\implies x = -6\n   \\]\n\n3. **Analyze the sign of the expression:**\n   Given that the denominator is always positive, we only need to analyze the sign of the numerator \\(x + 6\\) to determine where the entire expression \\(\\frac{x + 6}{(x + 1)^2 + 6}\\) is non-negative.\n\n   - For \\(x < -6\\), \\(x + 6 < 0\\), so \\(\\frac{x + 6}{(x + 1)^2 + 6} < 0\\).\n   - For \\(-6 \\le x < -1\\), \\(x + 6 \\ge 0\\) and \\((x + 1)^2 + 6 > 0\\), so \\(\\frac{x + 6}{(x + 1)^2 + 6} \\ge 0\\).\n   - For \\(x \\ge -1\\), both \\(x + 6 \\ge 0\\) and \\((x + 1)^2 + 6 > 0\\), so \\(\\frac{x + 6}{(x + 1)^2 + 6} \\ge 0\\).\n\n4. **Combine the intervals:**\n   From the analysis above, the expression \\(\\frac{x + 6}{(x + 1)^2 + 6}\\) is non-negative in the following intervals:\n   \\[\n   (-\\infty, -6] \\cup [-1, \\infty)\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the solution to the inequality \\(\\frac{x + 6}{x^2 + 2x + 7} \\ge 0\\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{(-\\infty, -6] \\cup [-1, \\infty)}\n\\]",
